  "faq": {
    "whatTitle": "What is the Forever Jukebox?",
    "whatBody": "The Forever Jukebox is an open-source modernization of Paul Lamere’s <infinite>Infinite Jukebox</infinite> and <canon>Autocanonizer</canon> — rebuilt from the ground up by <author>Creighton Linza</author>. It generates a forever-evolving version of any song.",
    "howTitle": "How does it work?",
    "howBody": "The app analyzes your provided audio to estimate beats, segments, and related features. Those features drive beat-synchronous playback. On each beat, the player may jump to a different, sonically similar point based on timbre, loudness, segment duration, and beat position. The visualization maps the possible jump paths.<br/><br/>The full source code is available in the <repo>forever-jukebox</repo> repository.",
    "tuneTitle": "How can I tune the Jukebox?",
    "tuneThresholds": "Open the Tune panel to adjust thresholds and branch probability.",
    "tuneTypes": "Set a minimum jump distance to filter branches by beat distance across the track, and use the checkboxes for other branch types.",
    "tuneDelete": "Select a branch in the visualization and delete it.",
    "storageTitle": "Where is analysis stored?",
    "storageBody": "Analysis is stored locally in your browser. You can clear individual track analysis on the Home screen, or all cached analysis by clicking the button below:",
